/* A 90-degree rotation of a real vector, (a,b) -> (-b,a), asked to reach the
   closed first quadrant infinitely often.  Valid: the rotation has period four,
   so every fourth step is in the quadrant, for any start but the origin -- which
   the query excludes.

   The certificate is a rank that is CONSTANT on each quadrant, 3/2/1/0 around
   the cycle, with no lexicographic structure: four pieces, one component.  The
   template can express that and the shape search cannot reach it, because its
   first growth step raises the lexicographic count to two and that count only
   ever increases, so a shape with several pieces and ONE component is never
   visited.  Set number_of_rfun_pieces to 4 in the well-founded predicate
   template and this answers valid in about 14 s; at 2 or 3 pieces it does not
   answer at all.

   The integer twin beside this file needs none of that -- over the integers a
   linear rank decreases by a whole unit for free, while over the reals the
   state can be scaled toward the origin so no linear form drops by the fixed
   one the decrease condition demands. */
Main 0
s.t.
Main (z:int): bool =mu forall (a:real). forall (b:real). (a = real(0) /\ b = real(0)) \/ Q a b;
Q (a:real) (b:real): bool =mu a >. real(0) /\ b >=. real(0) \/ Q (-.b) a;
